# Test [[.Name]]

Read `project.json` and `contracts_test.go`. Extend the existing `clitest.Case`
table rather than rebuilding HTTP servers and CLI output assertions for each
binding. Expected paths and query values come from the API contract, independently
of the manifest being tested. `Request: nil` asserts that no request occurs.

A successful case needs arguments, expected request and JSON response. Use
`Request.Query` (`url.Values`) for decoded query values, including spaces, `+`
and `&` when encoding matters. The helper supplies synthetic bearer auth, checks
the complete JSON result and preserves the configured `/admin` prefix. An error
case names both `WantExit` and `WantError`; a 503 fixture defaults to a private
body which must not leak into diagnostics.

For quick feedback run `go test -run '^TestCommandContracts$' -count=1 .`.
Verify a binding regression fails before the fix and passes afterwards. Keep
business assertions in the backend's existing tests: these fixtures prove client
mapping, not live server correctness. Use `go run . describe` for discovery.
Run `go vet ./...` and `go test ./...` before shipping; the latter also checks the
native executable. No real endpoint or credentials are needed.
